{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman of the Crusader states in the Levant He was born around 1140 and died in 1187 He was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 Raymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem He was also the nephew of Queen Melisende of Jerusalem\n\nRaymond III was still a child when his father died in 1152 and he inherited the county of Tripoli under the regency of his mother Hodierna He came of age in 1164 and took over the rule of the county He was known for his military prowess and played a significant role in the defense of the Crusader states against Muslim attacks\n\nIn 1164 Raymond III led a Crusader army to help the County of Edessa which was under attack by the forces of Nur ad-Din Zangi The Crusaders were defeated and Raymond III was captured and held prisoner for three years\n\nAfter his release in 1167 Raymond III returned to Tripoli and continued to defend the Crusader states He participated in several campaigns against the Muslim forces and was instrumental in the defense of the County of Tripoli during the Siege of Tripoli in 1171\n\nRaymond III also played a role in the politics of the Crusader states He was a rival of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and supported the claims of his cousin, Bohemond III of Antioch, to the throne of Jerusalem\n\nIn 1183 Raymond III was excommunicated by the Church for his opposition to the marriage of Sibylla of Jerusalem to Guy of Lusignan He was later reconciled with the Church but his relations with the other Crusader states remained strained\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin He was succeeded by his son, Bohemond IV of Antioch\n\n*Excommunication of Raymond III*\n------------------------------\n\nIn 1183 Raymond III was excommunicated by the Church for his opposition to the marriage of Sibylla of Jer